{"question_id": "V2-dev-BOOK-en-T2-002", "approach": "HYBRID+rerank", "approach_base": "HYBRID", "flags": {"rerank": true}, "setting": "en-en", "q_lang": "en", "d_lang": "en", "corpus": "K", "corpus_set": "BOOK_en", "answer_text": "Douglass learned to read in Baltimore first from Mrs. Auld, then through his own resourcefulness after the lessons were forbidden.\n\n- Mrs. Auld kindly began teaching him the alphabet and to spell words of three or four letters (Chapter VI).\n- Mr. Auld found out and forbade her to continue, saying it was unlawful and unsafe to teach a slave to read, that learning would spoil a slave and forever unfit him to be a slave, and that \"if you give a nigger an inch, he will take an ell\" (Chapter VI).\n- Douglass says the master's bitter opposition only increased his determination to learn, and he credits both the mistress's aid and the master's opposition (Chapter VI).\n- He finished learning by making friends of little white boys he met in the street, converting them into teachers; he took a book on errands, finished errands quickly to squeeze in lessons, and carried bread to exchange with the poor white boys (Chapter VII).", "abstained": false, "retrieved_units": [{"text": "[stripped: 886 chars]", "doc_id": "K.gutenberg-23_douglass_narrative.en", "section_id": "K.gutenberg-23_douglass_narrative.en::o005", "unit_ids": ["K.gutenberg-23_douglass_narrative.en::p1_b50"], "kind": "chunk", "score": 2.867188, "rank": 0, "span": [25277, 26163], "meta": {"chunk": 5656, "mode": "window", "partial_unit_ids": ["K.gutenberg-23_douglass_narrative.en::p1_b50"], "ranks": {"bm25": 7, "dense": 3}, "scores": {"bm25": 10.534632, "dense": 0.622674}, "rrf": 0.030798, "rerank": 2.867188, "rrf_rank": 2}}, {"text": "[stripped: 997 chars]", "doc_id": "K.gutenberg-23_douglass_narrative.en", "section_id": "K.gutenberg-23_douglass_narrative.en::o011", "unit_ids": ["K.gutenberg-23_douglass_narrative.en::p1_b109", "K.gutenberg-23_douglass_narrative.en::p1_b110"], "kind": 